                <h2>1. Build an Account-Tier Register</h2>
<p>The main challenge in multi-account management is not the number of accounts. It is knowing who owns each account, what it can run, and whether its balance and funding status are safe. Maintain one master register with one row per ad account. Record the platform, account name, account ID, legal entity, owner, tier, balance, funding date, objective, channel, creative folder, and notes.</p>
<p>Use internal account tiers rather than confusing them with official platform levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tier S: Core accounts</strong> have stable conversion performance and contribute significant revenue. Monitor balances, review status, and access changes closely.</li>
<li><strong>Tier A: Growth accounts</strong> have a defined testing objective and may scale within an approved budget. Confirm the balance, conversion target, and creative capacity before scaling.</li>
<li><strong>Tier B: Testing accounts</strong> are used for new creatives, audiences, or landing pages. Keep their budgets separate so test data does not distort core-account decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Tier C: Watchlist accounts</strong> have low consumption, weak conversion, or repeated risk signals. Restrict changes and require remediation before adding funds.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Pre-Launch Account Checklist</h2>
<h3>Access and Asset Isolation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assign one owner and one backup contact to every account to prevent simultaneous changes to budgets, targeting, and payment settings.</li>
<li>Maintain a separate creative library for each account. Do not directly copy unverified campaigns into multiple core accounts.</li>
<li>Distinguish ad accounts, asset platforms, and billing entities. Log every administrator change.</li>
<li>Do not attach testing accounts to core-account payment methods or place them in the same high-risk login environment.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Naming and Tracking</h3>
<p>Use a consistent format such as “Region_Product_Tier_Purpose_Date,” for example, “US_Tool_S_AI_Search_0310.” Maintain a mapping between internal names and account IDs so team communication does not lead to the wrong account.</p>
<h2>3. Funding and Balance-Alert SOP</h2>
<h3>Daily Checks</h3>
<ul>
<li>Review spendable balance rather than only the total balance. Mark pending charges, authorizations, and refunds separately.</li>
<li>Calculate the safe balance using daily budget multiplied by the expected number of consumption days. Do not rely solely on historical averages.</li>
<li>Set tiered alerts for Tier S accounts. Notify both finance and media buyers as spendable balance approaches a critical level.</li>
<li>Confirm that payments succeeded, currencies are correct, and exchange-rate or processing fees are included in reconciliation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Before Adding Funds</h3>
<ul>
<li>Verify the billing entity, account ID, currency, amount, and payment reference.</li>
<li>Check whether the account is under review, subject to a payment restriction, or consuming unusually quickly.</li>
<li>Avoid funding multiple accounts simultaneously without review. Allow manual approval time for large transactions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>After Adding Funds</h3>
<p>Store the order number, receipt, credit time, actual amount, funded account, and operator. Create an exception ticket for delayed credits, amount mismatches, or duplicate charges. Do not hide discrepancies by manually overwriting the balance sheet.</p>
